		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a165" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts328" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts329">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>You can say metamathematics and you can study the Cantian tradition where mathematics was basically carried out by means of constructions and is devoid of the idea of the information of a mathematical science being concentrated in the axioms and deduction as the method of deriving truths from truths already established.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a166" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts330" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts331">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>But the methodology of deductive sciences has a traditional and almost well it's not anti Kantian, but it's asserting the tradition in the face of the Kantian This methodology of deductive sciences is much more in the tradition of the American postulate theorists, Huntington, Devlin, the Italians that inspired them, Piano, Cottowa, The German's Passion, Hilbert Frege, Descartes, Pascal, it's something that reaffirms a tradition that goes back to Aristotle.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
